From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: SoloLuck ops <releases@sololuck.io>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 11:25:59 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 5/5] A solved block nobody answered about is UNRESOLVED, not
 rejected

Upstream ckpool never gave up on a solved block. generator_submitblock() spun on
cksleep_ms(10) until gdata->current_si came back, logging "No live current server
in generator_blocksubmit! Resubmitting indefinitely!". Crude, but it encoded the
right priority: a block is the only thing this software exists to produce.

The fan-out in the previous patch replaced that wait with one bounded thread per
configured bitcoind, so a hung primary can no longer sit on a solved block for
minutes. That was worth doing and stays. What went with it was the never-give-up
guarantee: with every bitcoind unresponsive for the SUBMIT_WAIT_MAX_MS window
(~44 s), generator_submitblock() returns false, and the stratifier calls
block_reject().

That is wrong twice. Nobody looked at the block, so "rejected" is a false record
of a verdict that was never delivered; and the block is then dropped with nothing
but a hex file on disk that no process reads.

  1. bitcoin.c can now say whether bitcoind actually ANSWERED.
     submitblock returns null (accepted), "duplicate" (already have it, also
     accepted), or a named reason -- "high-hash", "bad-txnmrklroot" -- which is a
     real rejection. No JSON, no root, no "result" is not a verdict, it is
     silence. submit_block_answered() reports the difference;
     submit_block_timeout() stays as a thin wrapper so no caller changes.

  2. generator.c keeps trying. The bounded fan-out is now block_submit_round(),
     callable repeatedly. When a round ends with no acceptance AND no verdict,
     generator_submitblock_ex() hands the block to a detached thread that runs
     more rounds on a 5s -> 60s backoff until some node accepts it or names a
     reason, or SUBMIT_RETRY_MAX_S (30 min) elapses -- and if it gives up it says
     so, with the exact bitcoin-cli line to replay the saved hex.

  3. stratifier.c gains a third outcome. local_block_submit() carries an
     *unresolved flag, and block_unresolved() records "submitted but UNRESOLVED"
     instead of a rejection. Seeing our own hash on chain still settles it as
     accepted; NOT seeing it settles nothing, because a node that answers
     getblockhash but never answered submitblock has told us nothing about our
     block.

Proven on the regtest rig (2 bitcoinds, ckpool -B, cpuminer), not argued:

  * Normal solve: block 205 accepted by both nodes in 9 ms, coinbase unchanged --
    25.0 BTC to the miner's own address, one spendable output, scriptSig still
    carrying "ckpool" and "/sololuck.io/".

  * Both bitcoinds SIGSTOPped -- TCP still accepting, nothing answering, which is
    exactly the failure this is about. Block 206 solved into the dark:
        Block submit to 127.0.0.1:18443 got NO ANSWER after 24.024s -- this is NOT a rejection
        Block 206 UNRESOLVED: no bitcoind returned a verdict. This is NOT a
          rejection; resubmitting in the background for up to 1800 s.
        Block 206 still UNRESOLVED after background resubmit round 1 (29s)
    SIGCONT, and round 2 landed it:
        BLOCK 206 ACCEPTED LATE: background resubmit round 2 succeeded 59s after
          the solve. The block was never rejected.
    Both nodes at height 206. block_reject() fired zero times in the whole run.
    On the old code this block is logged "Submitted, but had block 206 rejected"
    and lost.

  * Genuine rejection still rejects: a block with one flipped nibble in the
    coinbase, pushed through the generator, came back
    "SUBMIT BLOCK RETURNED: bad-txnmrklroot" and was refused with no retry storm.

Known limitation, stated rather than hidden: a block that only a LATE background
round lands logs "BLOCK ACCEPTED LATE" but does not re-run block_solve()'s
bookkeeping -- block_share_summary(), reset_bestshares(), the miner broadcast.
That state belongs to the stratifier's own thread. The block is on chain and paid;
the leaderboard reset is manual. In the test above the original caller was still
blocked inside generator_get_blockhash() when the nodes returned, so block_solve()
did run -- but that is luck, not design, and a longer outage would not have it.

Builds clean, no warnings.

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
